WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.660
Empty an island of every insect and spider.

2
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.000
How many species come back?

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:10.800
And are they the same ones?

4
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.400
In the Florida Keys, scientists did this to small mangrove islands.

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:23.160
Within about 250 days, most of the islands held about as many species as untreated

6
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.740
islands. Yet the species were not all the ones that had been there before.

7
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:34.273
A theory published in 1963 predicted this with two rates.

8
00:00:35.053 --> 00:00:36.613
The first is immigration.

9
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:41.213
That means the arrival of a species not already on the island.

10
00:00:41.953 --> 00:00:45.453
On an empty island, every arriving species is new.

11
00:00:46.093 --> 00:00:50.853
As the island fills, more arrivals belong to species already there.

12
00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:53.353
So the immigration rate falls.

13
00:00:54.003 --> 00:00:57.993
It reaches zero when every species in the pool is resident.

14
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:02.153
The pool is all the species that could reach the island.

15
00:01:02.953 --> 00:01:05.013
Our example uses invented numbers.

16
00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:07.977
The second rate is extinction.

17
00:01:09.157 --> 00:01:13.637
Here that means local extinction, the loss of a species from that island.

18
00:01:14.697 --> 00:01:20.077
If every resident species has the same, independent chance of disappearing, the

19
00:01:20.077 --> 00:01:23.517
extinction rate rises in proportion to the number of species.

20
00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:27.997
Draw both curves. They cross at one point.

21
00:01:28.577 --> 00:01:29.757
That is the equilibrium.

22
00:01:30.697 --> 00:01:32.877
In our example, it is 50 species.

23
00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:35.637
The crossing is stable.

24
00:01:36.597 --> 00:01:40.537
Above it, extinction beats immigration, and richness falls.

25
00:01:41.557 --> 00:01:44.917
Below it, immigration wins, and richness rises.

26
00:01:46.037 --> 00:01:47.617
Start with an empty island.

27
00:01:48.277 --> 00:01:50.957
The count climbs fast, then levels off.

28
00:01:51.657 --> 00:01:57.837
It reaches 45 species, 90% of the equilibrium, after about 11 and a half days.

29
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:02.550
At the crossing, five species arrive each day.

30
00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.550
Five species go extinct each day.

31
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.490
Net change is zero, so the count holds at 50.

32
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:11.850
But the species are changing.

33
00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.850
About five are replaced every day.

34
00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:19.310
In the Florida Keys, the second year showed the same pattern.

35
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.310
Species numbers changed little, while immigrations and extinctions continued at high

36
00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.450
rates. The model calls this a dynamic equilibrium.

37
00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:33.753
A count does not say which species are present.

38
00:02:34.613 --> 00:02:38.173
Take a selection of three mite groups from one mangrove island.

39
00:02:38.873 --> 00:02:40.933
Before treatment, two were present.

40
00:02:41.653 --> 00:02:43.533
A year later, two were present.

41
00:02:44.173 --> 00:02:48.493
But only one was on both lists, and three were found in either.

42
00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:54.913
To compare lists, divide the species they share by the species found in either list.

43
00:02:55.453 --> 00:02:57.213
That is the Jaccard similarity.

44
00:02:57.953 --> 00:02:59.293
Here it is 1/3.

45
00:03:00.193 --> 00:03:03.453
A steady count can hide a changing list of species.
